00:50:54.680 --> 00:51:24.960
<v Ira Glass>The song stylings of Mr. Anthony Newley here on This American Life . Act Three, "From the Audience Seats." OK, so you mean to go to the theater. That was so Baltimore, the way I just said that. OK, so you know you want to go to the movies. So you mean to go to the theater-- I'm from Baltimore so the accent creeps out sometimes-- so you mean to go to the theater. But it's so much trouble. And it's so much time. And tickets are expensive, and you have to pay the sitter, and there's parking, and yeah, yeah, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>安东尼·纽利先生的歌曲风格，就在《美国生活》节目中。第三幕，“观众席”。好吧，你是说你要去剧院。我刚才那样说太有巴尔的摩味儿了。好吧，你知道你想去看电影。所以你是说你要去剧院——我来自巴尔的摩，所以口音有时会流露出来——所以你是说你要去剧院。但这太麻烦了。而且太花时间了。票很贵，你还得付钱给保姆，还得停车，哎呀，没完没了。

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:50.920
<v Ira Glass>Mark O'Brien faces obstacles even more harsh than this to attend the theater. Because he had polio when he was a kid, he's in an iron lung. He can come out of the iron lung for a limited amount of time, though, and for a while-- as unlikely as this sounds-- he would use that time to go to plays. We offer this little piece of writing as an extreme example of what some people will go through, as audience, when they get the drama bug.
<v Ira Glass>马克·奥布莱恩为了去剧院，面临着比这更严峻的障碍。因为他小时候患过小儿麻痹症，他必须待在铁肺里。不过，他可以在有限的时间内离开铁肺，有一段时间——尽管听起来不太可能——他会利用这段时间去看戏。我们提供这篇短文，作为一个极端的例子，说明当有些人迷上戏剧时，他们会经历什么。

00:51:50.920 --> 00:52:29.740
<v Mark O'Brien>The first time I attended a performance of the Berkeley Shakespeare Festival, they did Hamlet in the round. It began when a man pushed a wheelbarrow on stage, [UNINTELLIGIBLE] through a trap door that trundled down a ramp three feet from me. The guards ran onstage dressed in Green Beret uniforms. I was hooked.
<v Mark O'Brien>我第一次参加伯克利莎士比亚戏剧节的演出时，他们表演的是环形剧场版的《哈姆雷特》。演出开始时，一个人推着独轮车上台，穿过一个活板门，在离我三英尺远的地方顺着坡道滚下来。警卫穿着绿色贝雷帽的制服跑上台。我被深深吸引住了。

00:52:29.740 --> 00:53:06.860
<v Mark O'Brien>After that, I went to every performance I could. The festival allowed a friend in for free. So every summer, I rented vans equipped with wheelchair lifts to take us to the park. The season's tickets cost more than $100, expensive for someone living on SSI. But as a friend told me, culture is what we live for, the reason we put up with bills, smog, and [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Mark O'Brien>那之后，我尽可能去观看每一场演出。戏剧节允许朋友免费入场。所以每年夏天，我都租用配备轮椅升降机的货车带我们去公园。季票价格超过100美元，对于靠社会安全补助金生活的人来说很贵。但正如一位朋友告诉我的，文化是我们活着的意义，是我们忍受账单、烟雾和[听不清]的原因。

00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:50.090
<v Mark O'Brien>The stage, surrounded by tall trees, faced a natural amphitheatre. I sat right next to the elevated stage [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. One of the light stands. One year, I bought two tickets for each performance. During a night performance of Coriolanus , the actor, the lead, dressed like a 20th century admiral, tore off his epaulets and hurled them offstage, nearly hitting me. The following week, the actor approached me before showtime.
<v Mark O'Brien>舞台四周环绕着高大的树木，面对着一个天然的露天剧场。我就坐在高台旁边[听不清]。其中一个灯架旁。有一年，我为每一场演出都买了票。在《科利奥兰纳斯》的夜场演出中，那位主演，打扮得像20世纪的海军上将，扯下他的肩章并将其扔下台，差点砸到我。接下来的一周，那位演员在开演前找到我。

00:53:50.090 --> 00:54:06.080
<v Mark O'Brien>"I'm surprised you're back," he said, "I thought I might've killed you with those epaulets." I was so excited to meet him that I forgot to ask for his autograph.
<v Mark O'Brien>“我很惊讶你又来了，”他说，“我以为我可能会用那些肩章砸死你。”我太兴奋能见到他，以至于忘了要他的签名。

00:54:06.080 --> 00:54:30.380
<v Mark O'Brien>Every performance was exciting because it was live and things might go wrong. Things seldom went wrong, but still there was always that chance. You [UNINTELLIGIBLE] that advantage from the deadly perfect land of movies and television.
<v Mark O'Brien>每一场演出都令人兴奋，因为它是现场直播，可能会出岔子。虽然很少出岔子，但总是有那种可能。你从电影和电视那种死气沉沉、完美无缺的土地上获得了那种优势。

00:54:30.380 --> 00:55:01.320
<v Mark O'Brien>I love seeing the same actor take on different roles. One actor, who imitated Johnny Carson, who played the clown, Touchstone, for As You Like It -- the following week he was Richard II, the tyrannical, blustering king who becomes more eloquent, more human, after he's overthrown and imprisoned.
<v Mark O'Brien>我喜欢看到同一位演员扮演不同的角色。有一位演员，他模仿约翰尼·卡森，在《皆大欢喜》中扮演小丑试金石——接下来的一周，他成了理查二世，那个暴虐、虚张声势的国王，在被推翻并入狱后，变得更加雄辩、更加人性化。

00:55:01.320 --> 00:56:10.800
<v Mark O'Brien>After many years in the park, the Berkeley Shakespeare Festival moved to a new amphitheatre in the suburbs. The stage area is half a mile from the entrance. When we reached our seats, I saw there wasn't a speck of shade. At 4:00 in the afternoon, it was well over 80. We often suffer for the things we love. This could be as true of Shakespeare as anything else. As an English major accustomed to the five-word sentences and flat inflections of Californians, I found Shakespeare's heightened sense of language intoxicating. It's not just the thees and thous. It's the metaphors, the jokes, the rhythms of the poetry and the jostling, bubbling, prose. So I thought I would suffer gladly for these pleasures.
<v Mark O'Brien>在公园里演了多年之后，伯克利莎士比亚戏剧节搬到了郊区的一个新露天剧场。舞台区距离入口有半英里远。当我们到达座位时，我看到一点阴凉都没有。下午4点，气温远超80度。我们经常为我们所爱的事物受苦。这对莎士比亚来说和其他任何事情一样真实。作为一个习惯了加州人五字句和单调语调的英语专业学生，我发现莎士比亚那高度发展的语言感令人陶醉。这不仅仅是那些“你”和“你的”古语。还有隐喻、笑话、诗歌的节奏以及那种推搡、活跃的散文。所以我认为我很乐意为这些乐趣而受苦。

00:56:10.800 --> 00:57:19.070
<v Mark O'Brien>I stayed in that blast furnace of language too long. The heat made me so weak that I couldn't drink any water. I became ill and had to be hospitalized. In the hospital, I clapped [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. I turned blue whenever the iron lung was off for more than three minutes. My doctor thought I would die. It took me six weeks to become well enough to leave the hospital. I told people I nearly became the first person to die from Shakespeare. It could be worse. Abraham Lincoln died from a lousy comedy.
<v Mark O'Brien>我在那座语言的熔炉里待得太久了。高温让我虚弱到无法喝水。我病倒了，不得不住院治疗。在医院里，我拍手[听不清]。每当铁肺关掉超过三分钟，我就脸色发青。我的医生以为我快死了。我花了六周时间才恢复到足以出院的程度。我告诉人们，我差点成为第一个死于莎士比亚作品的人。这还算好的，亚伯拉罕·林肯死于一场糟糕的喜剧。

00:57:19.070 --> 00:58:34.180
<v Ira Glass>Mike O'Brien lives in Berkeley, California. He's a writer and poet, author of a book of poems called Breathing . His email address, Marko-- that's M-A-R-K-O-- @well.com.
<v Ira Glass>迈克·奥布莱恩住在加州伯克利。他是一位作家和诗人，著有一本名为《呼吸》的诗集。他的电子邮箱地址是 Marko--也就是 M-A-R-K-O--@well.com。

00:58:34.180 --> 00:58:51.070
<v David Sedaris>I noticed my mother's face assume an expression she reserved for unspeakable horror. I had seen this look only twice before. Once when she was caught in the path of a charging, rabid pig and once again when I told her I wanted a peach-colored velveteen blazer with matching slacks.
<v David Sedaris>我注意到母亲的脸上露出了一种她留给无法言说的恐怖的表情。这种表情我之前只见过两次。一次是她差点撞上一头冲过来的疯猪时，另一次是我告诉她我想要一件桃红色的天鹅绒西装外套和配套的休闲裤时。

00:58:51.070 --> 00:58:56.070
<v Ira Glass>Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>下周回来，带来更多《美国生活》的故事。
